From: Liuqin Linux Kit contributors
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: liuqin: add CPU energy data and bandwidth monitors

Carry the OEM Cape dynamic power coefficients into the board CPU nodes.
qcom-cpufreq-hw already supplies the frequency/voltage OPPs from EPSS;
these coefficients allow its register_em callback to construct an Energy
Model without changing hardware frequency tables or fast switching.

Describe the missing CPU-to-LLCC BWMON v4 and LLCC-to-DDR BWMON v5 using
mainline icc-bwmon, OPP and RPMh interconnect bindings. The OEM Cape tree
and the pinned OS2.0.6.0 vendor_boot DTB agree on register addresses,
IRQs, the 19.2 MHz sampling timer and 64 KiB counters. The existing
sdm845 CPU and sc7280 LLCC implementations provide those register layouts
and counter units. Map v4's overlapping global/monitor regions as one
0x300-byte monitor window; the global IRQ registers land at +0x8/+0xc.

Convert the OEM LLCC/DDR frequency tables using their respective 16-byte
and 4-byte DCVS bus widths into decimal kB/s OPP votes. Keep the tables
on Liuqin rather than imposing its memory policy on every SM8475 board.
Use active-only votes, so the monitors do not impose sleep bandwidth.
Document the SM8475 compatibles with the existing driver fallbacks.

The OEM memlat policy and L3 DCVS controller are not copied: a memlat
threshold table is not a complete CPU OPP table, and feeding it to
qcom-cpufreq-hw would filter the firmware LUT and disable fast switching.
Existing PSCI domain idle states and regulator settings are retained.

Sources:
https://github.com/MiCode/kernel_devicetree/blob/1c507816ba8aa6117dcec41f577ef7a66d2091f7/qcom/cape.dtsi
OS2.0.6.0.VMYCNXM vendor_boot.img SHA256:
ec4c6543c254873bd6490ca01185c90cd11f01d99e16c70008923c13d73feab7

